# TOPAS 3000A — TypeScript / Node.js AsyncAPI Client

Runnable scenario snippets for consuming TOPAS 3000A AMQP 1.0
messages from Node.js (TypeScript), using the [`rhea`](https://github.com/amqp/rhea)
pure-JavaScript AMQP 1.0 client.

## Layout

- `sdk/` — **not present yet**: a typed TypeScript SDK is deferred to
  a follow-up sub-project. Each snippet parses incoming JSON
  with the built-in `JSON.parse` and casts to a small inline interface
  (or `unknown`); once the SDK ships, swap the cast for the generated
  type — or layer a [Zod](https://zod.dev) schema on top for runtime
  validation.
- `snippets/s1.ts` — subscribe to `{{ channels.detector_measurement }}` and print messages.
- `snippets/s2.ts` — subscribe to `{{ channels.wildcard_fault }}` (all faults across all domains; A8).
- `snippets/s3.ts` — publish a VMS DisplayMessage and await the correlated response (A4 + A6).
- `snippets/s4.ts` — distinguish sync vs delta messages and detect sync-batch completion (A3 + A5).
- `snippets/s5.ts` — subscribe to `{{ channels.traffic_signal_measurement }}` (DATEX-II 2017 profile).
- `snippets/s6.ts` — subscribe to `{{ channels.transport_link_measurement }}` and dispatch on the SCOOT/GPS/ANPR `kind` discriminator (F6).
- `snippets/s7.ts` — consume the static network topology across `network.{node,link,zone,path,turn}` and build an in-memory `Map`.

## Setup

The snippets target **Node.js 20+** and need one runtime dep
(`rhea` 3.0.5); JSON parsing uses the built-in `JSON.parse`, which is
in the language. Type-only deps for compilation: `typescript` 5.4.5
and `@types/node` ^20.0.0.

The canonical, smoke-tested project lives at
[`asyncapi_clients/smoketest/typescript/`](../../smoketest/typescript/) —
copy `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, and `tsconfig.json` from
there as the starting point. They pin `rhea` to the exact version
exercised in CI on every push, and the tsconfig's `include:
["../../output/typescript/snippets/*.ts"]` compiles all seven
snippets in one pass to `dist/snippets/*.js`. ESM (`"type":
"module"`) throughout.

Build:

```bash
cd asyncapi_clients/smoketest/typescript
npm ci          # one-off, deterministic from package-lock.json
npm run build   # tsc → dist/snippets/*.js
```

## Run

Set `TOPAS_AMQP_URL` to your broker (defaults to
`amqps://localhost:5671`). For local development against an Apache
ActiveMQ Artemis broker:

```bash
export TOPAS_AMQP_URL=amqps://localhost:5671

# Run a single snippet directly with tsx (no compile step).
npx tsx snippets/s1.ts
npx tsx snippets/s5.ts

# Or, with a recent Node, use the built-in tsx loader.
node --import tsx/esm snippets/s3.ts
```

If you prefer a pre-compile step:

```bash
npx tsc
node --enable-source-maps dist/snippets/s1.js
```

### Environment variables

| Variable | Default | Used by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TOPAS_AMQP_URL` | `amqps://localhost:5671` | all snippets |
| `TOPAS_MAX_MESSAGES` | `5` (S4 uses `20`) | S1, S2, S4, S5, S6 |
| `TOPAS_ORIGINATOR` | `asyncapi-clients-s3-demo` | S3 (sets `{{ headers.originator }}` per A4) |

The names match the Python, Java, C# and C++ snippets' env vars so
the docs stay consistent across languages.

## Don't hand-edit

The contents of `snippets/` are generated by
`asyncapi_clients/generator/generate.py`. Re-run with
`make asyncapi-clients` after changing `asyncapi.yaml` or any
template under `asyncapi_clients/templates/typescript/`.
